        title: 'Real-Time Systems & Deadlines',
        text: 'A **real-time system** is one where the correctness of a result depends on *when* it is delivered, not just *what* it is. Missing a deadline can be a glitch — or a crash.\n\n**Hard vs soft real-time:**\n- **Hard real-time:** missing the deadline is a system failure. Examples: airbag deployment, ABS braking, a pacemaker, engine control. A 1ms-late airbag is a dead airbag.\n- **Soft real-time:** occasional deadline misses degrade quality but are tolerable. Examples: audio playback (a drop-out), a video stream, a thermostat (a 100ms delay is fine).\n- **Firm real-time:** deadlines matter, but the system can recover with a degraded response.\n\n**The latency budget:** time from an event (sensor changes, interrupt fires) to the required action (actuator moves) is the **response time**. It includes:\n\n```\nevent -> interrupt latency -> ISR start -> ISR work -> main-loop/OS reschedule -> action\n```\n\n**Why this is hard:** a timer ISR might be blocked while another higher-priority ISR runs; the main loop might be mid-computation; a shared resource might be locked. Worst-case response time — not average — is what matters.\n\n**Tools of the trade:**\n- **Interrupts** with priorities let urgent work preempt the main flow.\n- **RTOS scheduling** (FreeRTOS) provides priorities and preemption for multiple tasks.\n- **Deterministic algorithms** — no unbounded loops, no heap allocation in the hot path.\n- **Worst-case analysis** — measure and bound the path from interrupt to action.\n\n**The professional mindset:** a real-time engineer asks "what is the worst case?" before "what is the average?". You design so the worst case meets the deadline, then leave headroom. That is the difference between a demo and a deployed safety system.',
        code: '// Worst-case response thinking:\n// ISR latency + ISR time + reschedule time must be < deadline.\n// ISR does the MINIMUM (set a flag / copy a value), heavy work\n// happens later in the background loop.\nvolatile uint32_t ticks = 0;\nISR(TIMER1_COMPA_vect) { ticks++; }        // 3 instructions, nothing more\nint main() {\n  while (1) { /* background: act on ticks when ready */ }\n}',
        note: 'Real-time = correctness depends on timing. Hard (airbag) vs soft (audio). Design for worst-case response, not average.',
