        title: 'The Embedded Development Environment',
        text: 'Embedded software is written on a host (your PC) and runs on a **target** (the MCU). The toolchain is a cross-compiler plus hardware tools for getting code onto the chip and seeing what it does.\n\n**The toolchain pieces:**\n- **Cross-compiler** — compiles C/C++ for the target CPU. For ARM Cortex-M: `arm-none-eabi-gcc`. For AVR: `avr-gcc`. It produces a binary the target\'s CPU understands.\n- **Binutils** — `objcopy`, `objdump`, `size`, `nm`: inspect and convert the binary, check flash/RAM usage.\n- **Linker + linker script** — places code, data, and stack at specific memory addresses defined by the chip\'s memory map.\n- **Flashing tool** — writes the binary to the chip: ST-Link (`st-flash`, `openocd`), AVRISP, JTAG/SWD probes.\n- **Debugger** — GDB connected over SWD/JTAG lets you set breakpoints, inspect registers, and see memory live.\n- **Build system** — CMake or Makefile orchestrating compile → link → flash → debug.\n\n**A typical embedded build flow:**\n\n```bash\narm-none-eabi-gcc -mcpu=cortex-m4 -mthumb -Wall -O2 \\\n  -T stm32f4.ld startup.s main.c -o app.elf\narm-none-eabi-objcopy -O binary app.elf app.bin\nst-flash write app.bin 0x08000000\n```\n\n**The hardware tools you\'ll meet:**\n- **JTAG / SWD** — debugging + programming ports on the chip. SWD is the modern 2-wire standard.\n- **UART** — the classic serial console: `printf`-style logging to your PC.\n- **Logic analyzer / oscilloscope** — to SEE the pins: timing, waveforms, protocol traffic.\n\n**The loop that matters:** edit → build (seconds) → flash (seconds) → debug (interactive) → repeat. A fast toolchain makes you productive; a broken flash tool makes you miserable. Master your toolchain first — everything else depends on it.',
